  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 855 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nWe had a limited area for potential storms this evening over\nsouthwest Iowa, but cloud cover kept temperatures slightly too\ncool for air parcels to be able to lift above the inversion in\nplace around 750-800 mb. We saw agitated cu try to develop, but\nnothing was able to take off. Now the shortwave has passed, \nprecip chances have dwindled, with clouds forecast to continue \nto break up across our area. More significant clearing expected \nover northeast Nebraska setting up an excellent scenario for \nfog development. This will expand south and east, more as low \nstratus overnight with reduced visibility still likely for areas\nalong and north of a line from Seward to Plattsmouth, east to \nRed Oak, IA. A Dense Fog Advisory may be necessary for a portion\nof northeast Nebraska, but it's uncertain at this time the \nextent of potential dense fog.\n\nMeanwhile, recent heavy rain has led to rises on local rivers.\nMaple Creek near Nickerson is cresting just around Minor Flood \nStage here at 8-9pm, so a Flood Warning was issued along this \ncreek through 10pm.\n\nZooming back out to the big picture, a broad upper-level ridge\nremains in place over the Central Plains and Mid-Mo River\nValley. Shortwaves inducing mesoscale vorticity maxima have \nbeen triggering our showers and storms the last couple of\nnights. The good news is tonight is looking pretty quiet, but\nthe next shortwave arrives Saturday morning bringing more\nshowers and weak storms to the region. Morning cloud cover\nshould limit the amount of surface-based instability, but steep\nlapse rates around 750-600 mb will create enough elevated\ninstability for a few storms to develop through the morning.\nStrongest activity with this MCV should stay down in northeast\nKS into northern Missouri through Saturday afternoon. More \nlikely we see scattered showers with a few embedded weak \nthunderstorms through the day, starting with the arrival of the \nMCV around 6am, diminishing mid-morning, and reigniting during \nthe afternoon around 3pm-ish clearing east by 7pm. \n\nAnother shortwave will trigger showers and storms along the\nelevated thermal front over southeast Nebraska and southwest\nIowa Saturday night into early Sunday. Not expecting anything\norganized as though we have plenty of elevated instability,\nshear through the storm layer is relatively weak.\n\nThe upper-level ridge shifts eastward on Sunday putting us in\nmore zonal southwesterly flow aloft. As this occurs, we see a\npush of northerly low-level winds finally clear the stationary\nboundary to our south during the afternoon. This will clear any\nlingering shower/storm chances and limit our daytime highs on\nSunday, only reaching the mid 70s north to low 80s south. \n\nWe stay mostly dry on Monday as the upper-level regime continues\nto shift to a more zonal flow pattern between the flattening\nridge to our east and building ridge over Baja California. The \nmilder Canadian air mass remains in place, keeping highs in the \nlow-to-mid 80s. We to see a slight chance of showers and storms \nin far northeast Nebraska Monday from potential nocturnal storms\nmoving in from Central Nebraska, but precip chances are only \naround 20 percent.\n\nTuesday into Wednesday will bring back a more active\nnorthwesterly flow pattern as ridging continues to build out\nwest. Better precip chances return to northeast Nebraska Monday\nnight into early Tuesday. A trough dips out of \nSaskatchewan/Manitoba bringing another cold front south through \nthe Dakotas into northern Nebraska Tuesday night/early \nWednesday. Models slightly differ on the behavior of this front,\nwith some stalling it across our area bringing a more extended\nperiod of showers and storms. Others clear it out to our south\nleading to a drier Wednesday afternoon into Thursday. In either\ncase, milder temperatures look to persist through the end of the\nweek with cooler canadian High staying in place across the\nregion going into the weekend.",
